label chapter_two_b:

    scene bg classroom

    narrator "The next day."
    narrator "You teach him capacitors."
    narrator "He already knows all of it."
    narrator "You know he knows."
    narrator "He knows you know."
    narrator "You teach it anyway."

    mc "A capacitor stores electrical energy in an electric field."
    mc "Two conducting plates separated by a dielectric."
    mc "Capacitance is charge per unit potential difference."
    mc "C equals Q over V."

    orion "Units."

    mc "Farads. Coulombs per volt."

    orion "Why is a farad impractically large?"

    mc "Because one farad means one coulomb of charge per one volt of potential."
    mc "One coulomb is already an enormous amount of charge."
    mc "Real circuits use microfarads. Picofarads."

    orion "So the unit is named after Faraday but almost never used at that scale."

    mc "Correct."

    orion "Doesn't that bother you?"

    mc "What?"

    orion "That we name things after people and then immediately make them irrelevant at that scale."

    narrator "You look at him."
    narrator "He's not joking."
    narrator "He's genuinely asking."

    thought "He thinks about things like this."
    thought "The naming of things."
    thought "The gap between what something is called and what it actually does."
    thought "I've never thought about that."
    thought "I've never thought about anything that wasn't going to be on the exam."
    thought "That thought sits in my chest like a stone."

    mc "I think Faraday probably didn't care what scale his name got used at."

    orion "Why not?"

    mc "Because he understood the thing itself."
    mc "The name was just administrative."

    narrator "He looks at you."
    narrator "That shift again."
    narrator "The one that isn't offense."

    orion "That's the first thing you've said that wasn't from a textbook."

    mc "I say things that aren't from textbooks."

    orion "Not about Physics."

    narrator "You open your mouth."
    narrator "Close it."
    narrator "He's right."
    narrator "You hate that he's right."

    thought "I've been treating Physics like a language I'm learning for a visa."
    thought "Not because I want to live there."
    thought "Just to get through the border."
    thought "He lives there."
    thought "He actually lives there."

    orion "Series and parallel. Derive the equivalent capacitance for both."

    mc "You're changing the subject."

    orion "I'm continuing it."

    narrator "You derive both."
    narrator "He watches."
    narrator "He doesn't correct you."
    narrator "That's almost worse."

